QtDMM Installation Instructions
(c) 2001–2014 M. Toussaint, 2025–2026 tuxmaster and contributors

QtDMM is a graphical front-end for digital multimeters based on the Qt 6
framework. Website: https://qtdmm.de


Requirements:
=============
- Qt 6 development files with the SerialPort, PrintSupport, Network, Svg
  and LinguistTools modules (Debian/Ubuntu: qt6-base-dev qt6-serialport-dev
  qt6-svg-dev qt6-tools-dev qt6-tools-dev-tools)
- optional: Qt 6 Connectivity (Bluetooth LE meters; qt6-connectivity-dev) and
  Multimedia (sound and video; qt6-multimedia-dev)
- CMake version 3.16 or higher
- hidapi (ubuntu: libhidapi-dev | fedora: hidapi-devel | FreeBSD: hidapi;
  Windows and macOS: fetched automatically)
- optional: python3 for the documentation checks in ctest, doxygen and
  graphviz for the developer documentation (./compile.sh doxygen)
- A supported or compatible multimeter - or none: the built-in virtual
  meter and the calculated values work without hardware


Compilation:
============
A helper script named `compile.sh` is included for convenience. You may use
it or follow the standard CMake workflow.

Basic steps:
------------
- Download and extract the QtDMM source code from GitHub:
  https://github.com/qtdmm/QtDMM
  (project website with handbook and downloads: https://qtdmm.de,
   contact: hello@qtdmm.de)
- Open a terminal and change to the QtDMM directory:
    cd QtDMM

- For a clean build (optional):
    ./compile.sh clean <other options>

- Build and install system-wide:
    ./compile.sh install
or
- Build only (no installation):
    ./compile.sh

- The compiled binary will be placed in the `./bin` directory:
    ./bin/qtdmm

FreeBSD:
--------
- pkg install cmake ninja pkgconf qt6-base qt6-serialport qt6-svg qt6-tools hidapi
  (python3 for the documentation check in ctest). CMake-generated Makefiles
  need GNU make (gmake), so either install gmake or use -G Ninja as below.
- Build, test and install:
    cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DBUILD_TESTING=ON
    cmake --build build
    ctest --test-dir build
    sudo cmake --install build             (prefix /usr/local)
  compile.sh works as well when gmake is installed.
- Serial ports are /dev/cuaU0, /dev/cuau0 ... and belong to group dialer:
    sudo pw groupmod dialer -m $USER
- USB-HID cables are opened through libusb; give your user access to the
  ugen device node with a devfs.rules entry, e.g. in /etc/devfs.rules:
    [localrules=10]
    add path 'usb/*' mode 0660 group dialer
    add path 'ugen*' mode 0660 group dialer
  and devfs_system_ruleset="localrules" in /etc/rc.conf.

macOS:
------
- Ready-made: the release page has a .dmg (macOS 13 or newer, Apple Silicon
  and Intel). It is signed ad hoc only, so macOS asks once before the first
  start - see docs/user/troubleshooting.md ("macOS").
- Building: brew install cmake qt@6, then ./compile.sh (it finds Qt through
  brew --prefix qt@6) or ./compile.sh run; hidapi is fetched and built by
  CMake, as on Windows. The result is build/qtdmm.app, for the Mac's own
  architecture. The release .dmg is built with the official Qt instead - see
  .github/workflows/cmake-mac-platform.yml.

Windows:
--------
- Visual Studio 2019 or newer (Qt 6 does not support MSVC 2017) and a Qt 6.5+
  msvc2022_64 kit; hidapi is fetched and built automatically by CMake.
- From a "Developer Command Prompt" with Qt's bin directory in PATH:
    cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DBUILD_TESTING=ON
    cmake --build build
    ctest --test-dir build
    cpack --config build/CPackConfig.cmake      (optional: portable ZIP and
                                                 Inno Setup installer in packages/)
  or open the folder in Visual Studio ("Open a local folder", CMake project).
- Ready-made builds: every push to master produces a QtDMM-windows-x64
  artifact in the GitHub Actions "Windows build" workflow.

special installations:
----------------------

Arch Linux:
curl -O https://raw.githubusercontent.com/qtdmm/QtDMM/refs/heads/master/PKGBUILD && makepkg --clean --syncdeps -fi

deb/rpm based Linux:
./compile.sh pack
will create a deb/rpm package. You can install this cleanly in your system.

Nix (Linux):
nix run github:qtdmm/QtDMM
starts QtDMM straight from the repository (flake.nix); `nix build` builds it
into ./result, `nix develop` opens a shell with everything to build and
test. The version shown is <year>.1-dev.<count>+g<hash>: the Nix source has
no git tags. On NixOS, add the flake as an input and the package to the
system, with its udev rule for the USB-HID cables:
  environment.systemPackages = [ qtdmm.packages.${pkgs.stdenv.hostPlatform.system}.default ];
  services.udev.packages = [ qtdmm.packages.${pkgs.stdenv.hostPlatform.system}.default ];
  users.users.<you>.extraGroups = [ "dialout" ];   # serial ports


Multimeter Compatibility:
=========================
If your DMM is not listed in the model selection (see README), try:
- Using manual configuration
- Selecting a similar model

Incorrect settings are unlikely to damage your device. In the worst case,
the multimeter may stop responding. Power-cycling it will usually fix this.


Feedback & Support:
===================
If you find working settings for an unsupported device or encounter problems,
please get in touch via the GitHub project page:

    https://github.com/qtdmm/QtDMM

